agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/crdt-wire
worktree: ../okay-wt-wire

why: the one item left open by stage 3 of
  specs/coordination-free.md — `Schema` for the wire, so a replica
  ships as data. It was named there rather than dropped, and this
  closes it.

what: `Schema` instances for `NodeId`, `Uid`, `Hlc.Stamp` and the five
  CRDTs, in okay-crdt (which gains a dependency on okay-codec; both
  are JVM + JS + Native, so nothing narrows).

  `Schema` has no Map and no Set — it has SInt, SLong, SString,
  SOption, SList, SVector, SProduct, SSum and SIso. That is not a gap
  to work around; it is what forces the interesting decision below.

THE PROPERTY THIS TURNS ON, and it is the reason the work is not
  mechanical: **equal values must encode to equal bytes.** A CRDT's
  state is maps and sets, whose ITERATION ORDER is not part of their
  value. Two replicas that have converged — that are `==` — would
  otherwise ship different bytes, and then every downstream thing
  that compares encodings (a cache key, a digest, a dedup check, a
  test) sees a difference that does not exist. So the wire form is
  SORTED, and that is a decision, not an implementation detail.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - **canonical encoding**: build the same value two ways so its
    maps and sets are built in different insertion orders, and
    require the encodings to be byte-identical. If they are not, the
    sort is missing or incomplete and the wire form is a lie.
  - **round trip**: decode(encode(x)) == x for every type, including
    the empty ones.
  - **the merge survives the wire**: merging two decoded replicas
    equals decoding the merge. If shipping loses information that
    `merge` needs — a tombstone, a per-node count — the encoding is
    lossy in exactly the way that breaks convergence, and only this
    law would catch it.

  The third is the one that matters and the one I would not have
  thought to write from the types alone.

predict: the sort is where the defect will be, specifically in
  `OrSet`, whose `adds` is a Map from an ARBITRARY `A` — I have no
  Ordering for it and will have to sort by something derived. That is
  also where a cross-platform difference could hide, since anything
  hash-based differs between JVM, JS and Native. Recorded.
